## **Vulnerabilities Remaining in 802.11i**

Even with all of 802.11i's improvements, some vulnerabilities remain:

**WPA2-Personal is vulnerable to offline dictionary attacks:**\
The PMK is derived from the password. The 4-way handshake can be captured. Offline brute-force can try passwords. The only defense is password strength.

**TKIP (if still in use) has known attacks:**\
Beck-Tews and Ohigashi-Morii attacks against TKIP's Michael MIC allow limited packet manipulation. Don't use TKIP.

**Management frames are still unprotected (without 802.11w):**\
Deauthentication attacks remain possible without Management Frame Protection (802.11w). 802.11w is optional in 802.11i/WPA2 but mandatory in WPA3.

**No protection against evil twin attacks:**\
In PSK mode, clients don't verify the identity of the AP — any AP broadcasting the right SSID with the right PSK will be connected to. 802.1X/EAP with certificate-based methods (EAP-TLS) provides AP authentication.

---

## **Summary**

802.11i is the complete IEEE standard for wireless LAN security, implemented as WPA2. Its architecture consists of: RSN negotiation (agreeing on security parameters during association), authentication (via 802.1X/EAP for enterprise or PSK for personal), a hierarchical key structure (PMK → PTK → TK/KCK/KEK and GMK → GTK), the 4-way handshake (establishing fresh session keys with mutual proof of PMK knowledge), and CCMP encryption (AES-based, providing both confidentiality and integrity). TKIP is a deprecated legacy option. Pre-authentication and PMK caching optimize roaming performance. WPA2 is the Wi-Fi Alliance's certification of 802.11i compliance, with CCMP as the mandatory cipher.
